many people across the country will be  struggling with their mental health with  lockdown meaning separation from loved  ones and potential anxiety about  returning to work we can speak now to  Paul farmer the chief executive of the  mental health charity mind very good to  see you this morning mr. farmer  how bad is it getting do we have any  idea of the effect that Kovach 19 and  indeed the lotto is having on the  nation's mental health yes where we are  concerned about an emerging mental  health crisis and in particular there  are three groups that we are paying  particular attention to first of all  people with existing mental health  problems where we know from our own  surveys that roughly two-thirds of  people with existing mental health  problems of reporting their mental  health is deteriorating secondly we're  concerns about those people our greatest  risk as a result of the kovat crisis  frontline workers people from black a  minority ethnic communities people may  be living in abusive households and we  know their mental health is at risk as  well and then finally of course as a  wider public we know that we've all been  asked to do things that are broadly  speaking not that good for our mental  health being separated from our loved  ones some of those protective factors  that we put in place to sustain our  mental health being removed so we do  have these three areas of concern which  I think all add up to a very significant  issue for our country's mental health  and as many people have perhaps at  taking news alluding to there has been a  focus on people's mental health  suffering through this process but as  you mentioned perhaps as many as one in  four of us had a pre-existing mental  health condition before corvid 19 came  along what has been the effect on the  access to mental health care provision  well I think the positive from the last  few years is the more people that be  more aware of their own mental health  and have sort out sort information and  support roughly a million people  downloading information from our website  in the last few weeks alone just to  think about how they look after their  mental house  but we do seem to be in this slight  paradoxical situation where would be  many people are reporting deteriorating  mental health but the NHS is reporting a  decline in numbers of referrals into the  mental health system so we do think it's  incredibly important to stress that  mental health services aren't very much  open for people to approach them there  is currently the capacity in there but  we are also in the middle of rebuilding  mental health services from a position  where really there was far too little  investment over a very long period of  time into building services as a fit for  the whole of all of us all up our whole  population and we're still on a journey  towards that so we think that services  are going to come under considerable  pressure and over the next few months  and it's absolutely vital that  appropriate action is taken now to put  in place the resources and the support  that's needed to cope with what's likely  to be quite a significant mental health  surge over the course of the next few  months we've been discussing on the show  this morning the idea that many children  may be going back to school on the 1st  of June and the lack of contact that  young people have been having with their  peers their social groups and all the  rest of it is bound to be having an  effect in your mind would it be better  for children to be back in schools  sooner rather than later because of that  mental health aspect well that's a  decision for government and local  authorities to make about on the grounds  of safety but from a mental health point  of view we know that young people are  missing their friends they're missing  the peer contact that they have  fortunately many are able to keep up  with their friends through social media  routes but I think the the quicker we  can get young people back into their  kind of normal cycle of activity the  better that will be for their mental  health looking after with support from  parents and teachers and professionals  and I think we're particularly wanting  to make sure that young people who do  have mental health problems are able to  access that help and support as quickly  as possible and the really effective  referral routes are put in place to make  sure that people can read  the mental health support they need  let's not forget that the number of  young people with mental health problems  was already on the rise before the kovat  crisis increasing from roughly 1 in 10  young people to now 1 in 80 on people  and so there's a real need to make sure  that we are highlighting the importance  of young people who may be developing  mental health problems getting access to  that help and if it's not possible to do  that through schools then other  mechanisms need to be found to make sure  those referrals are able to take place  so that young people get the help they  need particularly and I think we're  particularly concerned about young  people with emerging eating disorders  and increasing we've heard increasing  levels of anxiety and indeed loneliness  amongst young people and well many of us  myself included have had our wobbles and  our struggles and if people want more  information they can head straight to  mines website err but for now Paul  Farmer chief executive of the mental  health charity mind thank you so much  for joining us
 

